    lede: `Arrowhead Country Club's 27 holes run along the Waterway and Prestwick is Socastee High's home course. The backyard greens on the golf-adjacent streets around them share one problem with the club practice greens: pine straw and oak leaves that sit on the surface all fall and slow the roll by spring.`,
    question: 'Why is my Socastee backyard green slow and bumpy by spring?',
    answer: `Because pine straw and oak leaves from the same trees that line Arrowhead and Prestwick collect on an unblown green all fall and winter, packing into the fibers and the top-dress sand until the surface goes soft and uneven. TIMELESS Turf Restoration clears the green, fringe and collar, top-dresses with fresh green sand to depth, brushes it in and rolls the surface, then checks the speed, quoted by green size and condition.`,
    blocks: [
      { h2: 'Two golf clubs, one shared problem', html: `<p>Arrowhead Country Club's 27 holes run along the Intracoastal and Prestwick, the Pete Dye course, is Socastee High School's home track. The backyard greens in the golf-adjacent streets around both clubs sit under the same pines and live oaks the courses do, and they collect the same straw and leaves all fall. A green that isn't cleared goes slow and bumpy by spring for the same reason a neglected fairway does. <a href="/services/putting-green-restoration/">Putting green restoration</a> brings the speed back; blowing it off weekly keeps it there. Golf-lot yards in the streets nearest the Intracoastal, closer to Riverbend and Cypress River Plantation, tend to see it worst, since the Waterway adds its own humidity to what the pines already drop.</p>` },
      { h2: 'What builds up over a Socastee winter', html: `<p>Pine straw mats into a green's short fibers and holds moisture against them; live-oak leaves and catkins do the same in spring. Both work down into the top-dress sand if they aren't cleared, and a green that carries that load all winter has uneven, contaminated sand by spring, which is what makes the ball drift on one side of the green and hop on another. Waterway humidity keeps whatever collects there from drying out quickly, so it compounds faster here than on a green further inland.</p>` },
      { h2: 'The restoration', html: `<p>We clear the green, fringe and collar of debris, brush and loosen the compacted sand, clean the cups and fringe, top-dress with fresh kiln-dried green sand to depth, brush it in evenly and roll the surface, then check the ball's roll before we leave. Most backyard greens near Arrowhead or Prestwick fall in the same bands as a Premium Restoration; we quote the exact price by green size and condition on site. Because Brian's sister company builds greens, we can also say plainly whether a green that never seems to hold speed needs a maintenance visit or a rebuild.</p>` },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'Does living near Arrowhead or Prestwick mean more upkeep for my backyard green?', a: `Not more upkeep exactly, but the same trees that shed onto those courses shed onto your green too. Weekly blowing and an annual top-dress after the leaves and straw stop falling is what keeps the speed.` },
      { q: 'How often should a Socastee backyard green be top-dressed?', a: `Once a year for most, timed after fall leaf and needle drop has finished. Greens under heavy tree cover near the Waterway may need a lighter second visit in late spring.` },
    ],
