    title: 'Putting Green Restoration in North Myrtle Beach | TIMELESS',
    description: `Backyard greens at Barefoot Resort and Tidewater, cleared of pine straw and top-dressed by someone who builds them. Quoted on site.`,
    h1: 'Putting green restoration in North Myrtle Beach',
    lede: `Barefoot Resort's four championship courses and Tidewater's bluffside layout drew a lot of retirees who put a green in the backyard to practice. Those greens sit under pines and live oaks, and on the west side of the Waterway they're irrigated with Bull Creek water only, which the city's own report flags as higher in sodium.`,
    question: 'Why is my North Myrtle Beach backyard green slow and bumpy?',
    answer: `Because the pines and live oaks around Barefoot Resort and Tidewater drop straw and leaves onto backyard greens all fall and winter, and a green that isn't blown off regularly carries that load into spring, when it shows up as uneven, slow sand. TIMELESS Turf Restoration clears the green, fringe and collar, top-dresses with fresh green sand to depth, brushes it in and rolls the surface, then checks the speed. Brian has built and maintained greens for 13 years.`,
    blocks: [
      { h2: 'Retirees, four championship courses, a lot of backyard greens', html: `<p>Barefoot Resort has four championship courses, Norman, Love, Fazio and Dye, and Tidewater sits on the bluffs between the Waterway and Cherry Grove Inlet. The neighborhoods around both are full of retirees who put a green in the backyard to keep their short game sharp. A green built to perform needs the same firm, level sand a club practice green gets, which is what <a href="/services/putting-green-restoration/">putting green restoration</a> delivers. With a median age of 62 and more than four in ten residents over 65, this is a city with the time and the motivation to keep a short game sharp at home.</p>` },
      { h2: 'Bull Creek water on the west side of the Waterway', html: `<p>The city's own water quality report notes that addresses west of the Waterway, which includes most of the Barefoot Resort area, get Bull Creek water only, while the beach side gets a blend that includes a city well; the report flags the west-side water as higher in sodium. We don't treat greens with irrigation water, but we do check the heads and the surrounding sand on the inspection, since anything landing on the green repeatedly is worth knowing about.</p>` },
      { h2: 'What keeps the speed', html: `<p>A green that is blown off weekly and top-dressed once a year holds its speed; one that sits under straw all winter is slow and bumpy by spring. The restoration clears the green, fringe and collar, brushes and loosens the sand, top-dresses to depth with kiln-dried green sand, brushes it in evenly, rolls the surface and checks the ball's roll before we leave. Most Barefoot Resort and Tidewater greens fall in the same bands as a Premium Restoration; we quote the exact price on site. Because Brian's sister company builds greens, we can also tell you plainly whether a stubbornly slow green is a maintenance issue or a sign the base needs attention.</p>` },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'Does the water on the west side of the Waterway affect my green?', a: `We don't use irrigation water in the restoration itself, but the city's water report flags Bull Creek water, which serves that side, as higher in sodium than the beach-side blend. We check sprinkler heads and surrounding sand on the inspection as part of the visit.` },
      { q: 'How often should a Barefoot Resort or Tidewater green be top-dressed?', a: `Once a year for most, after fall leaf and needle drop has finished. Greens under heavy pine or live oak cover may need a lighter second visit in late spring.` },
    ],
